D={
"Gond":"The Gonds, numbering about 13 million, are among India's largest Adivasi communities, living mainly in Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Maharashtra, Telangana and Odisha. They speak Gondi, a Dravidian language. Gond kingdoms such as Garha-Mandla, Deogarh and Chanda ruled central India from the 14th to 18th centuries; Rani Durgavati of Garha died fighting Akbar's army in 1564. Their worship of Persa Pen and nature, their ghotul youth dormitories and vivid Gond painting, made famous by Jangarh Singh Shyam, remain central to their culture.",
"Bhil":"The Bhils, about 17 million people of Rajasthan, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh and Maharashtra, are India's largest tribal group. Famous archers, they fought alongside Maharana Pratap at Haldighati, and the Mewar coat of arms shows a Bhil warrior. They resisted British rule in uprisings led by figures such as Tantya Bhil. They speak Bhili languages, practise Pithora painting and celebrate Bhagoria before Holi.",
"Santhal":"The Santhals (Santals), about 7 million people in Jharkhand, West Bengal, Odisha, Bihar and Bangladesh, speak Santali, an Austroasiatic language written in Ol Chiki, created by Pandit Raghunath Murmu in 1925. In 1855–56 brothers Sidhu and Kanhu Murmu led the Santhal Hul against landlords, moneylenders and the British. Their faith, Sarna, worships nature spirits in sacred groves; President Droupadi Murmu is Santhal.",
"Munda":"The Mundas of Jharkhand, Odisha and Bengal speak Mundari, an Austroasiatic language. Their khuntkatti system gave land rights to the clan that cleared forest. Birsa Munda led the Ulgulan ('great tumult') of 1899–1900 against British rule and missionaries, and the Chotanagpur Tenancy Act of 1908 followed. They follow the Sarna faith and celebrate Sarhul and Karam.",
"Toda":"The Todas, a pastoral people of the Nilgiri Hills numbering about 1,500, herd sacred buffalo and live in barrel-shaped bamboo and thatch huts. Their dairy temples are tended by priests, and their embroidery (pukhoor) has a geographical indication tag. They practised fraternal polyandry historically.",
"Sentinelese":"The Sentinelese of North Sentinel Island in the Andamans are among the last uncontacted peoples, numbering perhaps 50–200. They reject outsiders, often with arrows, and India prohibits approaching within 5 nautical miles to protect them from disease. They hunt, fish and gather, and their ancestors probably came from Africa over 50,000 years ago.",
"Jarawa":"The Jarawa, about 400–500 people of the Andaman Islands, lived in isolation until 1998. They hunt pigs and turtles, fish with bows and gather honey. The Andaman Trunk Road through their forest exposed them to 'human safaris', which the Supreme Court restricted.",
"Great Andamanese":"Once about 5,000 people in ten groups, the Great Andamanese were devastated by colonial settlement, disease and violence; about 50 remain, resettled on Strait Island. Their languages are nearly extinct; the last speaker of Bo died in 2010.",
"Khasi":"The Khasis of Meghalaya, about 1.4 million, speak an Austroasiatic language and follow a matrilineal system in which the youngest daughter (ka khadduh) inherits property. They build living root bridges and organise in traditional states (hima) led by syiems.",
"Naga peoples":"The Naga peoples include over 16 tribes in Nagaland, Manipur and Myanmar, speaking Tibeto-Burman languages. Once famed for headhunting, which ended in the 20th century, most are now Baptist Christians. Morungs (youth dormitories), the Hornbill Festival and distinctive shawls mark their culture.",
"Apatani":"The Apatanis of the Ziro valley in Arunachal Pradesh practise sustainable wet-rice cultivation with fish in the paddies. Older women have facial tattoos and nose plugs. They follow the Donyi-Polo faith and celebrate the Myoko festival.",
"Bishnoi":"The Bishnoi community of Rajasthan follows 29 principles taught by Guru Jambheshwar in 1485, including protecting trees and animals. In 1730 Amrita Devi and 362 Bishnois died hugging khejri trees to stop the Jodhpur king's men cutting them, inspiring the Chipko movement.",
"Siddi":"The Siddis, about 50,000 people of African descent in Karnataka, Gujarat and Maharashtra, descend from Bantu people brought as soldiers, sailors and slaves. Malik Ambar, a Siddi general, ruled Ahmadnagar; the Siddis of Janjira ruled a sea fort.",
"Maria and Muria Gond":"The Maria and Muria of Bastar, Chhattisgarh, are Gond subgroups. The Muria ghotul trained young people in music, dance and responsibilities. Bastar Dussehra lasts 75 days.",
"Rabari":"The Rabaris are semi-nomadic camel and sheep herders of Gujarat and Rajasthan, known for mirror-work embroidery.",
"San (Bushmen)":"The San of the Kalahari are among the oldest continuous cultures, with genetic lineages diverging over 100,000 years ago. Their click languages, persistence hunting and rock art are famous. Many have lost land.",
"Maasai":"The Maasai of Kenya and Tanzania are cattle-herding pastoralists, famous for red shukas, jumping dances and warrior age-sets.",
"Aboriginal Australians":"Aboriginal Australians have lived in Australia for at least 50,000–65,000 years, the oldest continuous cultures, with Dreaming stories, songlines and rock art. Colonisation from 1788 caused massacres, disease and the Stolen Generations.",
"Inuit":"The Inuit of the Arctic hunt seals and whales, build igloos and use kayaks; they arrived from Alaska about 1,000 years ago.",
"Sámi":"The Sámi of northern Scandinavia and Russia herd reindeer and sing joik songs.",
"Ainu":"The Ainu are the Indigenous people of Hokkaido, recognised by Japan only in 2019.",
"Yanomami":"The Yanomami of the Amazon live in communal shabono houses; illegal gold mining threatens them.",
"Lakota":"The Lakota of the Great Plains, led by Sitting Bull and Crazy Horse, defeated Custer in 1876.",
"Māori":"The Māori reached Aotearoa (New Zealand) around 1250–1300; the Treaty of Waitangi (1840) shaped their relationship with Britain.",
"Bedouin":"The Bedouin are nomadic Arab herders of camels and goats.",
"Mongols":"Nomadic herders of the steppe, united by Genghis Khan in 1206.",
"Scythians":"Iranian-speaking nomadic horse archers of the steppe (9th–3rd century BCE), famous for gold art.",
"Etruscans":"The Etruscans of central Italy influenced early Rome.",
"Neanderthals":"Ice Age humans of Europe and western Asia.",
"Denisovans":"An Asian sister group of Neanderthals.",
"Homo floresiensis":"Small humans of Flores.",
}
